INT. DISUSED FUNICULAR CAR - NIGHT

A wooden car stalled halfway up a mountain track, lights guttering. Outside, black pines and a vertical drop. Inside, four people, a thermos, and a briefcase handcuffed to someone's wrist.

AGENT CLEMENTINE ROUX (40s, trench coat, the kind of face that gives nothing away) grips the briefcase handle. Across from her sits RODION VASILYEV (50s, fur collar, a gold tooth catching the light). Beside her, a young porter, FELIX (20s, uniform two sizes too big), shakes. At the rear window, a woman named SABINE HOLT (30s, driver's gloves, bruised lip) watches the track below.

CLEMENTINE
(to everyone)
Nobody touches the emergency brake.

RODION
It is already stuck.

FELIX
The cable's frayed. I told the foreman. I told him twice.

SABINE
(to herself)
Of course you did.

CLEMENTINE
Felix, how long until the next car?

FELIX
There isn't one. We're the last run.

RODION
Then we wait.

CLEMENTINE
(to the group)
We don't wait. Someone in this car cut that cable.

Sabine turns. Rodion leans forward. Felix presses against the wall.

RODION
You accuse us?

CLEMENTINE
I accuse the cable.

SABINE
(softly)
She is right.

Felix lunges for the door handle. Rodion catches his collar. Sabine grabs Rodion's wrist. The briefcase swings on its chain. Clementine slams it against the window, glass cracking, cold air hissing through.

For a moment, all four are tangled. Then the car lurches. Everyone freezes.

The sound of a single strand parting, far above.

CLEMENTINE
(low)
Nobody breathe.

Rodion lets go of Felix. Sabine lets go of Rodion. Slowly, painfully, they all sit down.

RODION
(to himself)
I told Anton we should have taken the road.

Outside, wind. Inside, the thermos rolls across the floor and stops against Felix's boot.
